DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ggeetthhoossttiidd() and sseetthhoossttiidd() respectively get or  set  a  unique  32‐bit
       identifier for the current machine.  The 32‐bit identifier was intended
       to be unique among all UNIX systems in existence.  This normally resem‐
       bles  the  Internet address for the local machine, as returned by ggeetthh‐‐
       oossttbbyynnaammee(3), and thus usually never needs to be set.

       The sseetthhoossttiidd() call is restricted to the superuser.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       ggeetthhoossttiidd() returns the 32‐bit identifier for the current host  as  set
       by sseetthhoossttiidd().

       On  success, sseetthhoossttiidd() returns 0; on error, -1 is returned, and _e_r_r_n_o
       is set to indicate the error.

